3.1 Orbital lines
There are thin cyan-blue geospatial orbit lines sweeping across the right side.

They are not ordinary decorative dividers.

They resemble satellite trajectories or geospatial data visualization paths.

Visible details:
- One large curved arc starts near the lower-middle/right region and curves upward toward the upper-right.
- Another thin diagonal line extends from near the moon toward the upper-right edge.
- Additional curved segments appear near the far-right edge.
- Small luminous points are positioned along some trajectories.
Use SVG paths for accuracy.

Example:
<svg
  class="orbital-overlay"
  viewBox="0 0 1671 941"
  preserveAspectRatio="none"
>
  <path
    d="M 945 495 C 1030 330, 1250 260, 1670 109"
    fill="none"
    stroke="rgba(0,180,255,0.55)"
    stroke-width="1.2"
  />

  <path
    d="M 1300 170 C 1420 155, 1530 125, 1670 109"
    fill="none"
    stroke="rgba(0,180,255,0.45)"
    stroke-width="1"
  />
</svg>


Add a subtle cyan glow to the SVG paths using:
.orbital-overlay path {
  filter: drop-shadow(
    0 0 4px rgba(0, 174, 255, 0.45)
  );
}


These graphics should remain very thin and unobtrusive.

